# @@NAME@@

A Rustman `@@POINT@@` plugin: @@DESCRIPTION@@.

It is a `no_std` Rust crate built into a WebAssembly component against the
`rustman:plugin` API through `rustman-plugin-sdk`. It imports nothing but the
Rustman host interfaces, so it gets no filesystem, network, environment,
clock or secrets beyond what a workspace grants it.

## Build

```sh
rustup target add wasm32-wasip2
cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2 --release
```

The component is `target/wasm32-wasip2/release/@@CRATE@@.wasm`, the
`component` of `rustman-plugin.toml`.

The first build records the exact `rustman-plugin-sdk` commit in
`Cargo.lock`. Commit it, and build with `--locked`, so every build of the
plugin uses the same SDK and dependencies.

## Test

```sh
rustman plugin test .
```

runs the cases of `rustman-plugin-tests.toml` in the real sandbox, with a
fake clock, randomness, environment, network and secrets, and exits 0 when
every case passes.

## Use

From the root of a Rustman workspace that contains this folder:

```sh
rustman plugin add ./@@NAME@@
```

`plugin add` pins the component's sha256 and declares the plugin with no
grants. Grant what it needs in its `[plugins.@@NAME@@]` table of
`rustman.toml` (@@GRANTS@@), then trust the workspace's plugin set.
